  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 224 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The cold front continues to move through the region this \nmorning, with dwindling rain showers and plentiful cloud cover along \nwith it. Rainfall amounts with this feature have generally been a \nfew hundredths, although some locations across northern New York \nhave observed a few tenths on an inch of rain. Strong cold air \nadvection will take place behind the front today, with increased \nclouds and some chances for some lingering showers in the favored \nupslope areas with some flurries possible in the higher terrain. \nTemperatures on Friday will be more seasonable compared to \nyesterday, with highs in the upper 40s and 50s. \n\nHeading into the weekend and the start of next week, surface high \npressure looks to build into the region once again. This will lead \nto another stretch of dry weather, with ample sunshine, light winds, \nand pleasant conditions to round out October and welcome the start \nof November. Temperatures on Saturday will be a little on the cooler \nside, with highs generally in the mid 40s to low 50s, moderating \ntowards the start of next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Breezy winds and lower relative humidity values will \nlead to increased fire weather concerns over the next few days, \nespecially given the plentiful dry leaf litter across the area \nserving as fuel. Wind gusts today and Friday will generally be in \nthe 15 to 25 mph range, with some higher gusts are possible. Minimum \nrelative humidity values will generally be between 40 to 55 percent \nfor today. Heading into the weekend, minimum relative humidity \nvalues should drop into the 30 to 45 percent range, but winds look \nto be much lighter. Recent rainfall amounts remain rather \nnegligible, with most locations only receiving a few hundredths of \nan inch, but a few locations across northern New York received over \n0.1 inches of rain with these showers. Cloud cover across the region \ntoday will help limit some of these fire weather concerns.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: There remains little change to the details regarding a \nfrontal boundary expected to shift southwards Monday night into \nTuesday. Upper level vorticity will shift east, and an incoming \nridge will help dissipate the front. It's very possible it does not \neven make it to the international border, but a handful of scenarios \ndepict it reaching at least far northern Vermont. For late Tuesday \ninto Wednesday, a surface low will lift from the Plains north \ntowards James Bay. It will send a warm front into the region. Better \nmoisture and dynamics will remain well to our north and west as \nsurface high pressure moving from far eastern Canada into the \nmaritimes will keep stable, southeasterly flow filtering into the \nregion. Again, some rain could develop with a handful of forecast \nscenarios depicting light rain along the international border.\n\nThereafter, the large scale pattern amplifies. The NBM is only \nforecasting lower 60s, but 925mb temperatures ranging from 10-13 C \nsuggest that some upper 60s could be in the cards. Overall, there's \nstill spread in terms of the evolution of a compact upper low across \nthe Plains, and how it interacts with a rich moisture plume off the \nAtlantic, but the timing is looking more consistent with an arrival \nof next Thursday night into Friday. This could be a very soaking \nrain. The NBM mean ranges from 0.50-1.00\", but the overall pattern \nsuggests we could see more. Seeing long range guidance with 1\" \nprobabilities of 25-40% and 2\" probabilities of 5-15% certainly \nsuggest some potential for a little more. We'll have plenty of time \nto monitor the evolution of this feature.",
